Publish the CPU count so commpage_populate() stops waiting for a kext.

ml_get_max_cpus() does not compute anything. It sleeps:

    if (max_cpus_initialized != MAX_CPUS_SET) {
            max_cpus_initialized = MAX_CPUS_WAIT;
            assert_wait((event_t) &max_cpus_initialized, THREAD_UNINT);
            (void) thread_block(THREAD_CONTINUE_NULL);
    }

and waits for ml_init_max_cpus() to wake it. Across the entire tree there is
exactly one caller of ml_init_max_cpus():

    iokit/Kernel/IOCPU.cpp:830, inside
    IOCPUInterruptController::initCPUInterruptController()

which is reached only when a platform driver constructs that controller. On a
shipping device the AppleARMPlatform kext does it. A kernel built from this
tree has no kexts, so nothing ever does, and the first thread to ask for the
processor count sleeps for the rest of the boot.

That thread is the one running kernel_bootstrap_thread. The path is
vm_commpage_init() -> commpage_populate() -> commpage_cpus() ->
ml_get_max_cpus(), and commpage.c flags the hazard on the line itself:

    cpus = ml_get_max_cpus();       // NB: this call can block

The symptom is a kernel that goes quiet with no panic and no reboot, after
IOKit has come up and while its matching threads carry on working -- which
looks exactly like a kernel that finished booting and had nothing more to say.

ml_parse_cpu_topology() has already parsed /cpus a few lines above, so the
count is known at that point and is the same one the platform driver would
have supplied. ml_init_max_cpus() is a first-writer-wins operation guarded by
max_cpus_initialized, so a platform driver that later calls it finds the work
done and returns without complaint.
--- a/osfmk/arm/arm_init.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/arm_init.c
@@ -291,6 +291,27 @@
 	master_cpu = ml_get_boot_cpu_number();
 	assert(master_cpu >= 0 && master_cpu <= ml_get_max_cpu_number());
 
+	/*
+	 * Publish the processor count now, from the topology the device tree
+	 * just gave us.
+	 *
+	 * ml_get_max_cpus() blocks on an assert_wait() until someone has called
+	 * ml_init_max_cpus(), and the only caller of that in the whole tree is
+	 * IOCPUInterruptController::initCPUInterruptController(), which a
+	 * platform kext is supposed to invoke. A kernel with no kexts never
+	 * does, so the first caller of ml_get_max_cpus() sleeps forever -- and
+	 * that caller is commpage_cpus(), reached from commpage_populate() by
+	 * way of vm_commpage_init(), with a comment in commpage.c that reads
+	 * "NB: this call can block". It blocks for good here.
+	 *
+	 * ml_parse_cpu_topology() has already read /cpus, so max_cpu_number is
+	 * the honest answer and is the same number the platform driver would
+	 * eventually supply. ml_init_max_cpus() only acts while
+	 * max_cpus_initialized is unset, so a later call from a real platform
+	 * driver becomes a no-op rather than a conflict.
+	 */
+	ml_init_max_cpus((unsigned int)(ml_get_max_cpu_number() + 1));
+
 	BootCpuData.cpu_number = (unsigned short)master_cpu;
 #if     __arm__
 	BootCpuData.cpu_exc_vectors = (vm_offset_t)&ExceptionVectorsTable;
